Yes. A modifier is a socket for authorial force.

“Good” names an evaluation.
“Very good” adds the author’s degree-setting on top of it.

That extra word is not neutral. It says, “I want you to read this farther along the scale.”

A more specific word can sometimes collapse the judgment back into the object-level description:

very good → excellent / masterful / precise / elegant / effective

That can make the sentence feel cleaner because the evaluation is carried by the chosen term rather than by a separate intensifier tugging on the reader.

So the rule becomes:

Use modifiers when you actually want a second channel.

For example:

* barely visible — threshold information
* technically correct — scope limitation
* strategically useful — frame selection
* probably wrong — confidence
* deliberately crude — intent
* unusually stable — comparison class

Those modifiers have jobs.

But:

really unique
very amazing
extremely incredible

usually just stack the author’s thumb onto the scale.

And your bias point is good: a separate modifier makes the persuasion layer easier to see and manipulate. It creates a little control surface where the author can turn intensity up or down without changing the underlying proposition.

So:

noun/verb/adjective = what I’m claiming
modifier = how I’m asking you to weight the claim

Sometimes that separation is useful. Sometimes it contaminates the sentence.

There is also a reverse trick: inventing or choosing a new word can strip inherited framing. Existing phrases come with old grooves cut into them. Rename the thing well and the reader has to inspect the pattern again instead of inheriting the previous author’s judgment.

That is why naming can be epistemic, not cosmetic.

A fresh word can remove somebody else’s modifier.

And once that happens, the thing gets a chance to speak for itself.